CAT = "capswPal"; SEG = "PAL"
# VLO 13: holistic, culturally safe and spiritually sensitive palliative and end-of-life care, death and bereavement.
Q = [
("What is the main goal of palliative care?",
 "Comfort and quality of life", ["Curing the illness", "Shortening the time left", "Moving the client to hospital"],
 "Palliative care aims to relieve pain and other symptoms and support quality of life for the person and family. It can start at diagnosis and run alongside other treatment."),
("A dying client no longer responds. How should you talk during care?",
 "Keep speaking and explain care", ["Stay silent to avoid disturbing", "Talk with coworkers instead", "Speak only to the family"],
 "Hearing is thought to be one of the last senses lost. Keep explaining what you are doing, and encourage the family to talk to the person."),
("Which is a common sign that death is near?",
 "Cool, mottled feet", ["A sudden fever of 40°C", "Increased appetite", "Faster healing of wounds"],
 "As circulation slows, the hands, feet and knees may become cool and mottled. Breathing changes, less urine and more sleep are also common."),
("Breathing that speeds up, slows, then pauses in a cycle near death is called:",
 "Cheyne-Stokes breathing", ["Kussmaul breathing", "Hyperventilation", "Normal deep breathing"],
 "Cheyne-Stokes breathing is a pattern of deep breaths that become shallow, then a pause. Report it; reposition the person for comfort."),
("A dying client's mouth is dry. What comfort care helps?",
 "Gentle mouth care", ["Large glasses of water", "Lemon juice on the lips", "Nothing, it is expected"],
 "Gentle mouth care, moist swabs and lip balm as directed ease dryness. Forcing fluids can cause choking when swallowing is weak."),
("A client says, \"Why me? It isn't fair!\" Which grief stage is this?",
 "Anger", ["Bargaining", "Denial", "Acceptance"],
 "Anger is one of the stages Kubler-Ross described: denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance. People may move between stages in any order."),
("A client asks you about medical assistance in dying (MAID). What should you do?",
 "Pass the request to the nurse", ["Tell her it is illegal", "Give her your own opinion", "Ignore it as a passing thought"],
 "MAID is legal in Canada and is assessed and provided by physicians or nurse practitioners. Respect the question, avoid personal opinions and pass it to the nurse or supervisor."),
("A family wants to follow a religious ritual at the bedside. What should you do?",
 "Support it within safety and policy", ["Refuse since it is not routine", "Ask them to wait until after death", "Only allow it if staff agree with it"],
 "Culturally and spiritually sensitive end-of-life care supports the family's rituals and beliefs, within safety rules and the plan of care."),
("What is the purpose of an advance care plan?",
 "To share wishes for care", ["To name who inherits property", "To cancel all current treatments", "To choose a funeral home"],
 "Advance care planning lets a capable person tell their substitute decision-maker and team their wishes and values for future care. Consent is still given by the person, or by their SDM if they become incapable."),
("Which is a sign of pain in a client who cannot speak?",
 "Grimacing and guarding", ["Calm, slow and even breathing", "Relaxed facial muscles", "Sleeping soundly"],
 "Facial grimacing, moaning, restlessness and guarding are pain signs in people who cannot say they hurt. Report them so pain can be treated."),
("A dying client refuses food. What is the best approach?",
 "Offer small amounts", ["Insist on finishing meals", "Feed them while they sleep", "Stop offering anything at all"],
 "Appetite naturally falls near death. Offer small amounts of what they like for comfort, but forcing food can cause choking and distress."),
("After a client dies, when is post-mortem care done?",
 "After death is pronounced", ["Before the family is told", "Only after the funeral home arrives", "Before the nurse is informed"],
 "Post-mortem care follows employer policy once death has been pronounced. The family may wish to see the person first, and cultural or religious practices may change the steps."),
("A grieving daughter cries at the bedside. What helps most?",
 "Stay nearby and listen", ["Tell her to be strong", "Say you know how she feels", "Leave the room at once"],
 "Being present and listening supports grief. Phrases like \"be strong\" or \"I know how you feel\" can feel dismissive."),
("Which position helps ease noisy breathing from secretions near death?",
 "On the side, head raised", ["Flat on the back", "Face down on the bed", "Sitting with the head forward on the chest"],
 "Side-lying with the head raised helps secretions drain and eases noisy breathing. Report it to the nurse, who may give medication."),
("Why might a PSW need support after a client's death?",
 "Grief affects caregivers too", ["Grief means poor professionalism", "PSWs should not feel loss", "Support is only for families"],
 "Caregivers form bonds and feel loss. Talking with the team, debriefing and employee assistance programs help prevent burnout."),
("What is hospice palliative care focused on?",
 "The person and family", ["The illness only", "The hospital's staffing needs", "Speeding up the dying process"],
 "Hospice palliative care treats the person and family as the unit of care, addressing physical, emotional, social and spiritual needs, including bereavement."),
]
